bound-lock/1 about="# @lapxo/bound\n\n## Use Bound in a project\n\nFrom a project with an admitted lock and selected views, inspect its standing, ask for its evidence, then verify the committed outputs:\n\n```sh\nnpx @lapxo/bound@0.1.5 fold --as lines\nnpx @lapxo/bound@0.1.5 fold --as receipts@1\nnpx @lapxo/bound@0.1.5 fold --check\n```\n\nUse this flow to review a project change or check a dependency's declared contract. `--check` verifies existing evidence; it does not execute a missing reading. A file fold names readings and explicitly says that it computed no encounters.\n\nFor observations from independent origins, use the typed cells view. In the shipped interval example, B's travelling ceiling is `0..50` and its travelling floor is `40..*`. A witnessed local join widens B to `0..100` while C and G keep the travelling ceiling `0..50`. Observations `42..48` and `44..47` on C meet at `44..47`; the native view reads FREE. Withdrawing one exact claim leaves one origin and reads REQUIRED.\n\nAfter cloning this repository, fold the example place with the instrument:\n\n```sh\nnpx @lapxo/bound@0.1.5 samples/typed\n```\n\nThe folder carries its own TARGET.bound, receipts.bound and hash-verified fixture data. It prints B, C, G and D through the native cells view; repeating the fold reuses their receipts. It needs no private key and admits nothing into your project. B owns the travelling sign, local join and travelling floor; C reads FREE from two compatible independent observations; G and D read REQUIRED. The artifact-set example uses the same instrument at `samples/artifacts`. [Typed records and sample](https://github.com/Lapxo/bound/blob/main/docs/contracts/typed-object.md).\n\nA selected form can instead describe a set of artifacts, a delivery interval or another domain's values. The project declares the form, origin evidence and views; Bound does not infer them from filenames or units.\n\n\nDeclare what must hold. Read what does. Keep the evidence.\n\nBound reads a project's admitted statements, checks observations against its requirements, and produces the views its selected topos declares. It keeps conflicting evidence and its origins instead of choosing a convenient answer.\n\n`fold` reads. `sign` signs a proposed lot. `land` admits that lot under the place's authority. These acts work across domains: a selected topos supplies forms, units, readers and views.\n\n## Start in an admitted project\n\n```sh\nnpx @lapxo/bound@0.1.5 fold --as help\nnpx @lapxo/bound@0.1.5 fold\n```\n\nThe help view prints:\n\n```text\nverbs fold land sign\nflags --as --check --key --key-file --signer --place\n```\n\nThe default fold runs the project's declared views. Its authority and selected topos determine what can be admitted, read and shown.\n\nStart with the team's accepted TARGET, admitted authority and selected readers. [Full usage guide](https://github.com/Lapxo/bound/blob/2053bb6d6188f04538114eae6a0add5166944ed3/docs/usage.md).\n\n## A meeting everyone can understand\n\nAna is available from 10 to 14, Luis from 12 to 16, and the room from 10 to 13. Their common availability is 12 to 13. A boss available from 18 to 20 makes the encounter incompatible. Withdrawing that exact reading restores the overlap; Ana, Luis and the room remain.\n\nWith an interval form, those are object operations: observe independent claims, meet them, withdraw by id, and read the state. A narrower compatible meet is not a fork. Folding a file of declarations alone does not calculate meeting availability.\n\nThe same pattern applies to a delivery window, a sensor's allowed range, or an agreed performance budget. The chosen topos determines the units and how evidence is obtained. Bound does not supply a meeting-specific rule.\n\n## See how information holds together\n\n![Cells, travelling bounds and a local join](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Lapxo/bound/2053bb6d6188f04538114eae6a0add5166944ed3/docs/readme-assets/atlas.svg)\n\nA travelling ceiling on B reaches C and G through their declared rest. A local join widens B alone. D is independent. The figure shows those relations; point size and camera distance do not measure information.\n\n| State | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| REQUIRED | Compatible bounds, fewer than two independent origins. |\n| FREE | Independent origins meet and their encounter fits the bounds. |\n| CONFLICT | Bounds conflict, or eligible claims have no common encounter. |\n| FORBIDDEN | An inhabited encounter does not fit the bounds. |\n\nBounds are checked before origins and claims. A receipt is evidence of a reading, not another origin. Duplicating one does not add freedom.\n\n## Change a project without erasing evidence\n\nWork in the project's directory. Use an existing admitted key identifier and its matching private key stored outside every repository. The admitted key also names its signer with `keys/<who> measure=signer value=file`; `signer/timeout` and `signer/response-bytes` declare its limits. Your public key and coverage belong to the authority contract; possession of a private key alone does not authorize a scope.\n\n```sh\nexport BOUND_KEY_ID='your-admitted-key-id'\nexport BOUND_KEY_FILE=\"$HOME/Private/keys/your-device.pem\"\nlot_dir=\"$(mktemp -d)\"\n```\n\nHave the selected view produce a proposal at `$lot_dir/proposal.bound`, or prepare the project's permitted lines there for review. Unsigned proposals use `by=target`, without a preassigned epoch or `sig`. Lots stay outside the repository; the committed bound files are only `TARGET.bound` and `receipts.bound`.\n\n```sh\nnpx @lapxo/bound@0.1.5 sign \"$lot_dir/proposal.bound\" \\\n  --key \"$BOUND_KEY_ID\" --key-file \"$BOUND_KEY_FILE\" \\\n  > \"$lot_dir/signed.bound\" &&\nnpx @lapxo/bound@0.1.5 land \"$lot_dir/signed.bound\" &&\nnpx @lapxo/bound@0.1.5 fold\n```\n\n`sign` assigns the admitted epoch and writes the signed lot to stdout. `land` validates the whole lot before admission. A refusal is evidence to inspect; it is not success. Do not proceed after a failed signature or admission. The key file is a PKCS8 private key matching the admitted public key; do not put it in the lot, source tree, package or CI artifacts.\n\nFor typed object withdrawals, `takes` names the exact claim or mark id. Removing a local join leaves the travelling sign live. For untyped configuration, use the wire's exact withdrawal selectors; do not combine withdrawal and replacement with identical selectors in one lot. Wire alphabet updates supersede by epoch.\n\n[Authority, withdrawals, receipts and PR review](docs/usage.md).\n\n## Read a declared view and reuse its evidence\n\nWhen the project has admitted a cells view:\n\n```sh\nnpx @lapxo/bound@0.1.5 fold --as cells\n```\n\nThe installed interval scene printed this on an unchanged second reading:\n\n```text\nRECEIPTS 4 read · 0 opened · idle · provider 0\n```\n\nNative receipts identify the live semantic inputs, declared rest, selected topos, codecs and selected rendering implementation. They exclude delivery count, display paths and Bound's error wrapper. A local join changes its own cell's receipt. A travelling sign changes dependent cells. Missing or corrupt carried bytes cannot prove reuse.\n\nFor judgement:\n\n```sh\nnpx @lapxo/bound@0.1.5 fold --check\n```\n\nKeep stdout, stderr and the exit status. `--check` is not an install-and-build replacement. A source test passing does not pay an unread ceiling, prove a cold clone, or accept a release.\n\n## Select a topos by what it declares\n\n`sources/<name>` says where a world can be obtained. An admitted `uses/<name>` names the digest of that topos's folded standing: canonical live claims in wire byte order, excluding signature envelopes and host shape. Re-signing the same standing preserves the pin. A changed live form, class, view or offer changes it.\n\n`restsOn` closes artifact bytes separately. A capsule is one offer. Forms, classes and views need no capsule merely to be selected. Runtime and packing do not define standing identity. npm dependency ranges remain package metadata; they are not standing pins.\n\n| Responsibility | Owner |\n| --- | --- |\n| Meet, state, live acts and rest | Obligations |\n| Wire, forms, classes, views and offer contracts | Topos |\n| Admission, folding and evidence | Bound |\n| Domain measurement and representation | Selected topos |\n| Storage, transport and execution | Host |\n\nCell `restsOn` is the object's relation. Directory prefixes and file summaries are host coordinates, not object rest. Wire tokens are protocol constants, not organization, host, path or key literals.\n\n## Repository rules\n\nCommit `TARGET.bound`, `receipts.bound` and the outputs the forge reads. Ignore `.bound/` from the first line of `.gitignore`. Keep proposals, signed lots, keys, ledgers, caches and dependency blobs outside the published tree.\n\nThe lock is the project's own contract and pins. Read a dependency's declarations from its selected standing; do not copy its lines into your lock. README, package metadata and workflows must be renders of admitted declarations. A scan is an observation, not permission to lower a ceiling.\n\nA PR proposes lines and the leaf changes they render. The judge uses the team's accepted contract independently of the proposed revision. Preserve refusals, conflicting origins and unpaid demands. [Repository and contribution rules](https://github.com/Lapxo/bound/blob/2053bb6d6188f04538114eae6a0add5166944ed3/docs/usage.md#repository-and-pr-review).\n\n## An atlas you can question\n\nInformation can share a history without sharing a single presentation. The same admitted cells can support a compact status view, a spatial view of their relations, or a focused reading of one disagreement. Each view asks for a region and resolution; its selected topos determines the representation.\n\nThis makes changes inspectable. A local act can remain local, travelling bounds can reach dependent cells, and a receipt can name the inputs of the reading you saw. You can ask what changed and what depends on it without turning a drawing into the algebra.\n\nA pin names standing, not a download URL or runtime entry. Its declared artifact dependencies close the bytes needed for an offered operation. The boundary lets a form or view remain meaningful even when it has no executable capsule.\n\nUse Bound where independently attributed observations must be judged against an accepted contract: compatibility between components, operational limits, or a team's project requirements. The selected topos must supply the actual measurements; a digest identifies the evidence without declaring it true.\n\n[Contribute a counterexample or a proposed lot](CONTRIBUTING.md).\n\n\n## Foundations and related libraries\n\nThe two-sided constraint object is described in Nahum Ochoa's [Two-Sided Constraints on a Valued Lattice](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21858428), DOI `10.5281/zenodo.21858428`. Bound uses the object through [Obligations](https://github.com/Lapxo/obligations); [Topos](https://github.com/Lapxo/topos) supplies the wire and contracts for forms, views and selected worlds. Bound admits, folds and retains evidence across those declared boundaries. A digest identifies bytes; it does not certify that an observation is true.\n" at=witness:formed-native-samples by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/public-0 shape=README.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Authenticated evidence view\n\nThe declared evidence@8 view projects authenticated delivery history of the place. It preserves signatures and key attribution, including admitted receipts from an attest-class key. Evidence grants neither normative authority nor independent observation origins. Existing Topos authority and coverage remain the admission boundary.\n\nA sample is a place: fold its declared evidence view with the instrument. The native-view vector invokes only Bound: bootstrap with an external host signer, sign and land evidence, inspect help, lines and native receipts, inspect authenticated evidence, and redeliver retained signed bytes. No private key, registry or validation executor is shipped. The vector names its external signer parameters explicitly; absence cannot be replaced by a fabricated signature.\n\nThe rejected sample contains an uncovered write and altered signed bytes. Both refuse by name. Withdrawals remove only covered normative lines; authenticated delivery remains inspectable. Aliases of one key grant no independence. Native receipt fixture bytes are retained output from the instrument, not observations written by a test.\n" at=witness:native-evidence-sample by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/contract-evidence-view shape=docs/contracts/evidence-view.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Contributing to Bound\n\nBring a counterexample, an observation or a proposed lot. Include the inputs and the output that led you to it. A signature establishes who was authorized to speak; it does not make their result true.\n\n## Set up a working copy\n\nClone the repository and use the Node version supported by package.json. If package-lock.json is present, run npm ci; otherwise use npm install --ignore-scripts --no-package-lock. Then run:\n\n```sh\nnpm run build\nnpm test\n```\n\nThese are development checks, not project acceptance. For a place with admitted trust context and native receipts, run the product judgement as well:\n\n```sh\nnpx @lapxo/bound@0.1.0 fold --check\n```\n\nPreserve a refusal and its exit status in your report. Do not borrow a neighbouring checkout's ledger or substitute local bytes for a named pin.\n\n## Make the smallest useful change\n\nBound owns admission, folding and evidence. Topos owns grammar, forms, classes, views and their contracts. A selected topos owns domain measurement. Obligations owns the algebra. Keep each change in its layer; do not put a domain decoder, path-based inference or a fallback for a missing pin into Bound.\n\nAn algebra change needs an independent oracle and a counterexample. A host change needs evidence for authentic bytes, failure propagation or reuse. A view change needs its declared region and implementation identity. A test should fail for a plausible incorrect implementation, not assert a source string or repeat the same expression on both sides.\n\nKeep regressions for changed signatures, unauthorized coverage, exact-id withdrawal, conflicting evidence, local versus travelling acts, corrupt content and duplicate delivery. Consolidate repeated setup and duplicate scenarios without removing distinct falsifiers.\n\n## Propose and admit\n\nKeep proposal and signed-lot files outside the repository. Use the current admitted wire, by=target, and no preassigned epoch or signature. The team's admitted key signs the lot; Bound admits it under scope coverage. The [usage guide](docs/usage.md) explains the transaction and external key-file requirements.\n\nSend the proposed lines with the leaf changes they render. A hand-edited render is evidence of the intended change, not an admitted contribution. Do not edit the accepted TARGET to make a test or ceiling pass. If a render disagrees with the lock, fix the proposed lines and re-render it.\n\nCommit only the project's TARGET.bound and native receipts.bound as bound files. The first .gitignore line excludes .bound/. Do not commit keys, stores, ledgers, caches, capsules, archives or vendored dependencies.\n\n## Submit a pull request\n\nExplain the concrete behavior before and after the change, the affected layer, and the checks you ran. Include stdout, stderr and exit status for a CLI case. State which reader and input bytes produced the evidence; a source harness is not an installed-package proof.\n\nPolicy changes receive explicit team review separately from measurements of the proposed source. Keep incompatible readings and their origins visible. Never remove a failing test or weaken a ceiling to obtain acceptance. Withdraw statements through their admitted history; do not rewrite prior signatures, tags or releases.\n\nDocumentation and workflows follow the same review: propose their lines and include the rendered changes.\n" at=policy:public-prose by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/public-1 shape=CONTRIBUTING.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Exact release artifact\n\nAn archive digest names its exact bytes. It is distinct from a Topos standing identity. The pipeline produces one archive for the registry and forge; it does not pack a second file and assume equality.\n\nThe public conformance vector uses only the installed instrument. Two unrelated formed places resolve their named standing and artifacts, then print their cells. A changed artifact with the retained digest refuses before execution. This proves the host materialization boundary; it does not claim that file presence is an installed-release receipt.\n\nAcceptance of the candidate additionally requires its exact member list, archive hash, clean installation and product captures from that archive, without source conditions or a checkout ledger. Changed implementation bytes require a new candidate capture. Packing, installing and publishing are host acts; the vector does not authenticate to a registry or publish. The owner approves that exact archive after verification.\n" at=witness:native-artifact-sample by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/contract-archive shape=docs/contracts/archive.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Explanation — historical profile\n\nThe declared view selects idea, what-holds and refuses from the byte-pinned historical document provider. The place owns its language and prose. Idea renders its own heading and body. The other two regions require their declared law readings or captured refusals; absent evidence supplies no invented result. The executable sample uses two unrelated places, checks the complete explanation file, repeats the product fold without a file difference and asks the named view directly. It also refuses a well-formed view asking an unoffered region. This quartet proves own-prose projection and absence under this profile; it does not certify law readings, a rejection corpus, typed-object states or the pending general document contract. Bound does not parse this prose.\n\n# README — historical profile\n\nThe declared view selects hero@3, commands@3 and idea@3 from the byte-pinned historical document provider. The place supplies its name, tagline, language, sentence rule and idea. Commands and measured badges are absent in the sample: no command, number or success is invented to fill them. Two unrelated places check the generated README, a second unchanged product fold and the named view; a declared unoffered region refuses by name. The implementation member is verified against the admitted historical artifact. This quartet proves the selected prose profile, not command execution, badges with measured values or a general document grammar. The eight text@3 demands remain unpaid.\n\n# Citation file — historical profile\n\nThe declared view asks the pinned document provider for cff at resolution 3. It projects the place name, version and optional citation metadata into CFF text. The sample uses the exact implementation member from the admitted historical artifact, names its hash and license, and exercises two unrelated places through the compiled CLI. It checks complete citation bytes and two equal readings; title and version follow each place. An unoffered region refuses by view name. Missing optional metadata is omitted; missing name can produce an empty answer under this historical profile. Rendering is not verification of a scientific claim or an external DOI. This pinned profile does not implement the pending general document contract.\n\n# Guide — historical profile\n\nThe declared view asks seq from the pinned historical document provider. The place owns page/seq lines and their heading. Canonical input order supplies the steps; the provider numbers that order and writes the requested shape. The executable sample uses two unrelated procedures, checks their complete Markdown and reads each view twice without differences. A declared view with an unoffered region refuses by name. An empty step set may have an empty answer; it is not invented evidence. A rendered instruction does not prove that instruction was executed. The implementation bytes remain the existing pinned profile, not a document grammar inside Bound.\n\n# Reference — historical profile\n\nThe declared view combines term, set and ref from the pinned historical document provider. The profile projects the place term and meaning, its selected world names and links to its declared Markdown shapes. The executable sample runs two unrelated places, checks their own term and meaning, the actual selected world and relative guide link, and captures two identical CLI readings. A view asking an unoffered region refuses by name. Missing terms or links can yield empty component answers; these are not measurements or a claim that obligations were paid. These historical regions and selectors belong to the selected Topos; Bound supplies verified inputs and dispatch only. The general document contract remains unpaid.\n\n# Manifest render profile\n\nThis declared view uses the selected verbatim-prose provider at text@3. The place assigns its complete manifest text to its output shape; Bound verifies and dispatches the selected offer and writes that shape. The provider does not infer a package schema, dependency graph or fields from the filename. The sample renders two places with distinct JSON bodies twice and checks exact file bytes. Two distinct prose bodies assigned to the same shape refuse; earlier outputs remain unchanged. This proves rendering, not that arbitrary JSON is an installable package.\n\n# Workflow render profile\n\nThis declared view uses the selected verbatim-prose provider at text@3. The place owns the workflow text and its shape; Bound does not infer a forge or manufacture pipeline steps. The sample writes distinct YAML bodies in two places, reads the rendered files twice and compares exact bytes. Conflicting bodies for one shape refuse without replacing prior outputs. This is a render contract, not evidence that a forge executed, staged or published a release. The release pipeline has its separate artifact gate.\n\n# Usage render profile\n\nThis declared view uses the selected verbatim-prose provider at text@3. A prose line with measure=text and the requested shape supplies its own body. The sample renders distinct Markdown bodies in two places twice and compares exact bytes. Multiple identical bodies are one text; differing bodies for one shape refuse. It invents no sections or documentation schema. This existing profile does not pay the eight general-document demands or validate commands that prose happens to mention; executable commands require their own captured vectors.\n\n# Build configuration — historical profile\n\nThe declared tsconfig view asks the admitted package provider for its build region. It projects the place's build/* lines whose shape matches the requested file: true and false become JSON booleans, other option values remain strings, and include separates its declared alternatives. The byte-fixed fixture names the implementation member and its artifact independently; Bound does not interpret compiler options. Two unrelated places exercise the compiled CLI, check the complete generated configuration and two byte-identical readings. A declared view asking an unoffered region refuses by name. This profile renders configuration; it does not validate compiler options or claim a successful build. An empty option set renders an empty compilerOptions object. It is a pinned historical profile, not the pending general document contract.\n" at=witness:public-contracts-by-operation by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/contract-documents shape=docs/contracts/documents.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Help view\n\nA declared help view projects the instrument verb and flag table. It is generated from that table, not copied prose or a claim that another world is supported. Asking it does not authorize a line or call a selected external reader. The sample declares view/help value=help, checks the printed three verbs and refuses a declared view whose region has no provider.\n\n# Lines view\n\nA declared lines view projects canonical normative standing as wire lines. It is a configuration reading; it does not compute encounters or turn evidence into authority. Live exact withdrawals disappear from normative standing while authenticated evidence remains inspectable through its own view. The sample verifies signatures and attribution, redelivery ONCE, exact withdrawals, tampering and uncovered writes. A declared missing provider refuses rather than returning an invented reading.\n\n# Receipts view\n\nThe native receipt view projects the fold through the admitted Topos receipt contract. The sample uses the published place profile receipts@8|receipts@0|receipts@1: observations, summary and current region bytes. These are that profile’s declared projections, not a universal enumeration of resolutions. A receipt identifies the actual observed set; it is not an origin or a valuation. The sample obtains a native receipt, verifies its signed delivery from an attest-class key through evidence, and checks idempotent delivery. The refused sample names an uncovered write, tampered signature and declared view without a provider. No harness-written JSON is used as a receipt.\n\n## Public verification\n\nFull-resolution native reading receipts carry the verified canonical UTF-8 input closure in the bytes row and the verified output in the count row, through the wire’s existing about field. Count, digest and measured duration keep their existing meanings. The host verifies the supplied bytes against those digests before reuse; an altered supplied carrier is not replaced by a cached copy. Historical local-only receipts still require their authentic CAS bytes. No private ledger or store is needed to verify a complete public receipt.\n\nFile-region seals hash configuration through Topos’s existing publication projection. Admission envelopes and retired history stay in the authority ledger; live declarations and ordinary file bytes remain inputs. Object history uses its own contract. A public checkout must carry receipts made for its actual files and compiled reader, not seals copied from a different tree. Verification does not run readers, fetch dependencies, refresh receipts or grant authority.\n\n# Why a coordinate\n\n`bound fold <coordinate> --as why` explains the live declarations whose exact `needs` member or `shape` names that coordinate. It prints the selector, witness and value. It does not compute an object encounter or infer authority from a path. The published sample bootstraps two independent places, explains each declared source coordinate and prints `OPEN` for a coordinate no declaration names. That absence is not zero, green or a state. A declared view with no provider refuses by name with a nonzero status. Calling why without a coordinate is not this sample’s acceptance vector; its empty historical capture pays nothing.\n\n# Declared programs\n\nThe programs view asks the selected Topos demand-status projection at resolution 8. Selection, minimum, evidence, elapsed reading and time ceiling coordinates belong to that Topos contract; Bound only hands admitted declarations and region receipts. Program status is derived from that evidence and the declared dependency graph. A demand with no evidence remains unread, its dependant remains blocked, and a missing elapsed reading keeps deadline unread. No cell encounter, origin count or information quantity is computed by this view. The sample uses the published SDK implementation on two unrelated demand chains, captures equal readings twice, names the next unblocked coordinate, and refuses dependency cycles. It does not provide stage conformance evidence or claim either program closed.\n\n# Demand card\n\nThe declared card view asks the selected Topos demand-card projection at resolution 8. It reads the selected demand coordinates, their explicit dependencies and the handed evidence region under selectors declared by that Topos. It does not infer progress from files or convert a declaration into proof. A requirement without evidence is unread; an unpaid predecessor blocks its dependant. The next coordinate is an unblocked unpaid demand. Deadline is unread when no elapsed receipt is handed; absence is not zero. These labels describe program evidence, not Obligations cell states. The sample pins the implementation member from the published Topos SDK, folds two unrelated places twice with equal stdout and refuses a declared dependency cycle. This sample intentionally has no evidence to close either program; it certifies the projection of absence and order, not stage acceptance.\n" at=witness:public-contracts-by-operation by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/contract-views shape=docs/contracts/views.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Published contracts\n\nEvery public surface names its contract, accepted sample, captured executable vector and rejected sample. Missing members, differing artifact digests and failed vectors remain unpaid. Publication conformance is distinct from installed-artifact acceptance.\n\nThe selected Topos projects each quartet as a reading through the existing wire. Execution proofs are dependency readings; identical vector bytes share one proof. Each reading names its exact handed inputs and dependent receipt digests. Bound verifies those closures and keeps native count/bytes receipts by digest. A closed reading costs no new execution. The publication aggregate reads the quartet receipts; it never starts an inventory batch.\n\nExecution slots, per-execution timeout and response bytes are declared resource lines in the selected Topos. Bounds remain unchanged. A failed reading is not cached as a successful proof. Earlier completed readings stay verified, so a later refusal does not force them to execute again. Changed, missing or corrupt bytes cannot reuse their receipts. Output and execution history retain their exact coordinates.\n\n## Act cost\n\nEach declared reading and each host invocation is measured separately. The total duration of a pass is a diagnostic; it is not the cost of one indivisible act. The cost/act ceiling reads the largest measured act or the remaining unpartitioned host work. Nested invocations are not charged twice. A slow or refused act retains its cost. Native reading receipts retain measured seconds separately from semantic identity; reusing a receipt performs no new reader execution. Missing historical durations are not invented as zero.\n\n## Public verification\n\nFull-resolution native reading receipts carry the verified canonical UTF-8 input closure in the bytes row and the verified output in the count row, through the wire’s existing about field. Count, digest and measured duration keep their existing meanings. The host verifies the supplied bytes against those digests before reuse; an altered supplied carrier is not replaced by a cached copy. Historical local-only receipts still require their authentic CAS bytes. No private ledger or store is needed to verify a complete public receipt.\n\nFile-region seals hash configuration through Topos’s existing publication projection. Admission envelopes and retired history stay in the authority ledger; live declarations and ordinary file bytes remain inputs. Object history uses its own contract. A public checkout must carry receipts made for its actual files and compiled reader, not seals copied from a different tree. Verification does not run readers, fetch dependencies, refresh receipts or grant authority.\n" at=witness:public-receipts-verify-without-private-store by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/publication-contract shape=docs/contracts/publication.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Using Bound\n\nThis guide covers the current CLI in a place whose wire and authority are already admitted. A fresh unsigned TARGET is a proposal, not a trusted project. The prepared repository is not yet a certified empty-clone bootstrap.\n\n## Before the first fold\n\nYou need the team's accepted TARGET and trust context, authentic bytes for each selected digest, and the readers/views that TARGET names. Do not borrow a neighbouring repository's ledger or copy node_modules to make a missing pin look available.\n\nThe published repository carries exactly TARGET.bound and receipts.bound as bound files. Local admission history lives in the ignored .bound/ store. Published standing excludes the signature envelope; owner public keys retain their signatures.\n\nStart by asking for help and folding the project. If a selected view is unavailable, use the named refusal and its fix; do not invent an output with a similar name.\n\n## Keys and coverage\n\nUse a named key already admitted by the project. The authority contract declares its public key, class and scope coverage. Signing uses a declared port. The lock names `keys/<who> measure=signer value=<name>`; `signer/timeout` and `signer/response-bytes` bound each invocation. The built-in `file` port reads a matching PKCS8 PEM outside the repository through `--key-file`. For a command port, the host supplies a `BOUND_SIGNERS` registry mapping that admitted name to `{ \"kind\": \"command\", \"executable\": \"...\", \"args\": [] }`. A repository declaration cannot authorize a program. `--signer <name>` must match the admitted selection.\n\nA new device must have its public key and coverage admitted by an authority that already covers the intended scope. The CLI does not currently certify silent key creation or tap-to-sign. This guide does not substitute a self-authorized fixture key for the team's authority.\n\nFor automation through the file port, supply the private key through the runner's secret storage to a temporary external file with restricted permissions. A command signer receives the lot once as canonical signed bytes, one line per record in order. It returns one canonical wire response per record, containing `by` and `sig`, in that same order. Bound verifies the entire reply against the admitted public key before any admission. Timeout, nonzero exit, missing or invalid replies refuse the whole lot; raw signer output is not logged. Hosts can keep private keys outside Bound through this port. Key generation, storage and rotation belong to the host. Do not print, commit or upload it. A source-control token and a Bound authority key have different purposes. Key rotation and revocation are changes to the admitted authority contract, not edits that erase prior signatures.\n\n## Proposal → signature → admission → reading\n\nPrepare the proposed lines outside the repository. Each line must use the current wire's fields, forms and required set. Configuration retains its legacy required fields, including `at`. Typed cells, claims and marks use their exact row grammar; `role=reads` or `at` cannot repair an incomplete typed record.\n\nA proposal names `by=target` and omits epoch/signature. The CLI assigns epoch from admitted history when signing. Never copy an epoch from an example or change a signed line's body.\n\nUse the signing and admission transaction in the README. Shell `&&` prevents a failed sign from being followed by land. Successful signing alone does not mean admission. Successful admission alone does not mean every requirement is paid. Fold after land and inspect its states, receipts and exit status.\n\nCLI sign authenticates wire inscriptions. It is different from the object's travelling ceiling sign. The selected Topos decodes spans and supplies origin/witness context; Bound must not guess those from by, widens or a path.\n\n## Reading and withdrawing\n\nAsk only for a view admitted by the project, such as cells. A local join widens that cell with a witness; it leaves the travelling ceiling live. A travelling require supplies a floor. Claims carry distinct origins; their signatures do not by themselves establish independent origins.\n\nFor typed histories, a negative record names the exact id in takes. Withdrawing a claim removes that live observation; withdrawing a join removes that local widening, not the sign. A fresh claim with the same content is a new identity, not resurrection of a withdrawn id. Re-delivery of the same signed bytes is idempotent.\n\nFor configuration, the negative line preserves the exact non-envelope selectors and changes value to withdraw. Do not withdraw and replace identical selectors in the same batch. Use a new epoch when superseding an alphabet declaration. Never withdraw evidence merely to obtain a green verdict.\n\n## Receipts and refusals\n\nreceipts.bound is native fold output. Do not write it by hand, use JSON from a test as a receipt, or copy receipts from another tree. Unchanged inputs can reuse verified region receipts; missing bytes require recomputation or a refusal. A duplicate receipt does not create another origin.\n\nREFUSE·wire means the current grammar does not admit the record. REFUSE·pin/release means named authentic content or its closure is unavailable or does not match. REFUSE·view means the requested view cannot be rendered under its contract. Authority refusal means the signer is not covered. Keep the line and the failed exit status; follow an explicit fix without changing the named digest or dropping a conflicting statement.\n\nrelease/blocks is policy read by the fold. Its admitted category links select existing obligations. The fold prints each missing reading or crossed ceiling as BLOCK; a blocked or incomplete policy is not READY. Read the captured verdict for the exact candidate instead of inferring acceptance from a successful render.\n\n## Repository and PR review\n\nThe accepted TARGET is independent of a contributor's scan. Commit native receipts and admitted renders, not local .bound/ state. A page disagreeing with TARGET must be re-rendered; a ceiling is not lowered to fit the page.\n\nA contribution is a proposed lot with the leaf changes rendered from it. Send that lot for admission by the team's coverage. Do not submit hand-edited lock output as already accepted. Check secrets, extra bound files, foreign vendoring and uncaptured page claims before review.\n\nA review includes the product fold, meaningful suites and verification of the rendered bytes. Preserve their actual output and exit status.\n\n## Declaring a demand\n\nA new demand records what remains to be proved. Admission does not create conformance evidence or pay it. An OWED line after land says the declaration was admitted and remains unpaid. Signing authority and conformance evidence are separate inputs.\n\nThe selected program projection reads declared requirements and handed evidence. Missing evidence stays unread; a missing elapsed-time reading is not zero. A dependency cannot close before its requirements and predecessors. Declaring a plan never certifies a release.\n" at=witness:declared-demand-close by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/public-2 shape=docs/usage.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Walk\n\n`bound fold --as walk@<resolution>` compares the admitted place with an authenticated peer inventory. Resolution is interpreted by the declared Topos selection and projection contract; it is not restricted to three fixed numbers. The wire declares region membership, context, origin and cuts. The output names touched regions, open regions and payload bytes; protocol and imported bytes carry their own units.\n\nForeign history retains its signed origin epoch. Sender keys authenticate that history and never grant coverage or independent origins in the receiver. Whole-region prefixes are verified before ingress. Import and the receiver-signed local receipt commit atomically; only that receipt advances the receiver's local epoch. Foreign withdrawals are confined to the same authenticated origin and exact identifier. A repeated valid delivery adds no lines and retains its existing import receipt. A rejected or interrupted ingress adds neither evidence nor a receipt.\n\nThe published sample uses the same compiled public reader on measurement and artifact places. It compares equal summaries, opens a differing region, exchanges at intermediate declared resolutions, converges by union, retains disagreement and verifies replay after a later local act. It rejects tampered payloads, missing records, unauthorized signatures, non-prefix epochs and undeclared context changes. It interrupts a real receiver signer and verifies unchanged ingress and local history. Dead-owner recovery is a separate host action; this view does not silently recover it. The typed object's origin-local claims and withdrawals are a separate conformance vector, not inferred from configuration counts.\n" at=witness:publish-native-walk-contract by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/view-walk shape=docs/contracts/view-walk.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# fold\n\n`bound fold` reads the admitted place. Configuration output names readings and says no encounters computed; its counts are not independent-origin states. A declared cells view projects admitted typed history into Obligations. A selected world defines other views; its contract decides when empty output is valid. A refusal or nonzero status wins over any parseable diagnostic stdout. `--check` verifies receipts without inventing a missing reading. The published evidence vector folds fresh places, preserves admitted key attribution, reads a native receipt and checks exact withdrawals. The typed-object quartet covers object states separately.\n\n# land\n\n`bound land <lot>` admits authenticated lines through the same boundary used by signing and previews. Authority, coverage, historical wire and typed context remain distinct checks. Unknown wire and tampered signatures refuse. Exact redelivery returns ONCE; it does not append another origin or grant new authority. Withdrawals remain exact under their declared contract. A refusal does not append a partial act. The published evidence vector lands fresh places, refuses unauthorized writes and tampering, redelivers signed bytes and exercises exact withdrawals. The signing-port vector additionally checks interrupted or invalid command responses do not land a partial lot.\n\n# sign\n\n`bound sign <lot>` returns canonical signed lines after validating the whole lot. The admitted key selects a declared signer; `--signer` selects the same port explicitly. File and command mechanisms use one port. Topos signing-lot/1 frames canonical bytes in lot order, one invocation per lot, and returns signatures and public-key IDs. Verification checks the admitted public key, independently of the mechanism. A command is invoked without a shell and under declared bounds. No secret output is disclosed in refusals. Missing, invalid, partial, timed-out or failed responses refuse; `land` cannot commit their partial output. The sample compares file and command signatures and landed lines, checks invocation count and ledger hashes, and refuses an unknown wire version. The sample uses ephemeral keys outside the place; no owner key is included.\n" at=witness:public-contracts-by-operation by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/contract-cli shape=docs/contracts/cli.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="## Typed cell, claim and mark records\n\nThe typed profile is selected by admitted wire/object/* declarations in Topos. A cell names its selected form, parameter bytes, topos standing and declared restsOn coordinates. Positive ceilings travel; a witnessed join is local; positive floors travel. Claims carry independently admitted origin evidence. Bound projects the admitted history into Obligations and prints its four states; configuration readings do not compute those encounters.\n\nEach sample is a place with TARGET.bound, native receipts.bound and fixture data whose hashes the lock names. After a clone, run `npx @lapxo/bound@0.1.5 samples/typed`; `samples/artifacts` demonstrates another form through the same path. No sample runner or private key is required. The native vector folds the signed histories for compatible claims, both CONFLICT causes, FORBIDDEN, exact withdrawals and reuse, and refuses floor/local. It compares the CELL lines with the directly constructed Obligations scene.\n\nA history artifact digest closes its exact bytes; it is not the identity of the place. Digest-only place selection waits for a semantic projection that preserves effective act order while excluding delivery envelopes. No archive or generic standing encoder is substituted for that contract.\n" at=witness:formed-native-samples by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/contract-typed-object shape=docs/contracts/typed-object.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about=", & " at=policy:bound/010 by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/cite/and value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1200\" height=\"760\" viewBox=\"0 0 1200 760\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"title desc\">\n<title id=\"title\">One history, several views</title><desc id=\"desc\">Illustrative spatial view of B, C, G and independent D. C rests on B and G rests on C. B's travelling ceiling is 0 to 50 and travelling floor 40 to 100. Its local join widens only B to 0 to 100. Region receipts retain selected topos and implementation identity. Background points are decorative, not additional measured cells.</desc>\n<defs>\n<radialGradient id=\"bg\"><stop stop-color=\"#163445\"/><stop offset=\"1\" stop-color=\"#080f1c\"/></radialGradient>\n<radialGradient id=\"dot\"><stop stop-color=\"#fff\"/><stop offset=\".4\" stop-color=\"#b4f8ed\"/><stop offset=\"1\" stop-color=\"#2c8c9a\"/></radialGradient>\n<linearGradient id=\"edge\"><stop stop-color=\"#62d9c4\"/><stop offset=\"1\" stop-color=\"#8da4ef\"/></linearGradient>\n<filter id=\"glow\" x=\"-100%\" y=\"-100%\" width=\"300%\" height=\"300%\"><feGaussianBlur stdDeviation=\"12\"/></filter>\n<marker id=\"arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 10 5 0 10\" fill=\"#7ce4d1\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<style>text{font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,'Segoe UI',Arial,sans-serif;fill:#eef5fa}.muted{fill:#aac0ce;font-size:16px}.small{fill:#aac0ce;font-size:14px}.label{font-size:19px;font-weight:600}.mono{font-family:Menlo,Consolas,monospace;font-size:15px;fill:#8fe7d7}</style>\n<rect width=\"1200\" height=\"760\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"url(#bg)\"/>\n<text x=\"64\" y=\"68\" class=\"small\" letter-spacing=\"3\">THE ALGEBRA IN 60 SECONDS</text>\n<text x=\"64\" y=\"118\" font-size=\"38\" font-weight=\"600\">One history. Several views.</text>\n<text x=\"64\" y=\"152\" class=\"muted\">A cell keeps its readings. A connection carries only what travels.</text>\n\n<path d=\"M687.2 493.6 Q622.5 471.6 557.7 519.6\" stroke=\"#588d9e\" stroke-width=\".6\" opacity=\".13\" fill=\"none\"/>\n<path d=\"M687.2 493.6 Q611.8 400.4 536.4 377.1\" stroke=\"#588d9e\" stroke-width=\".6\" opacity=\".13\" fill=\"none\"/>\n<circle cx=\"687.2\" cy=\"493.6\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#8cafbc\" opacity=\".4\"/>\n<path d=\"M557.7 519.6 Q599.3 438.0 640.9 426.5\" stroke=\"#588d9e\" stroke-width=\".6\" opacity=\".13\" fill=\"none\"/>\n<path d=\"M557.7 519.6 Q694.0 469.6 830.3 489.7\" stroke=\"#588d9e\" stroke-width=\".6\" opacity=\".13\" fill=\"none\"/>\n<circle cx=\"557.7\" cy=\"519.6\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#8cafbc\" opacity=\".4\"/>\n<path d=\"M640.9 426.5 Q679.9 444.8 718.9 533.1\" stroke=\"#588d9e\" stroke-width=\".6\" opacity=\".13\" fill=\"none\"/>\n<path d=\"M640.9 426.5 Q532.8 423.4 424.6 490.3\" stroke=\"#588d9e\" stroke-width=\".6\" opacity=\".13\" fill=\"none\"/>\n<circle cx=\"640.9\" cy=\"426.5\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#8cafbc\" opacity=\".4\"/>\n<path d=\"M718.9 533.1 Q593.9 462.3 468.9 461.5\" stroke=\"#588d9e\" stroke-width=\".6\" opacity=\".13\" fill=\"none\"/>\n<path d=\"M718.9 533.1 Q723.2 406.5 727.6 349.8\" stroke=\"#588d9e\" stroke-width=\".6\" opacity=\".13\" fill=\"none\"/>\n<circle cx=\"718.9\" cy=\"533.1\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#8cafbc\" opacity=\".4\"/>\n<path d=\"M468.9 461.5 Q624.8 408.6 780.7 425.6\" stroke=\"#588d9e\" stroke-width=\".6\" opacity=\".13\" fill=\"none\"/>\n<path d=\"M468.9 461.5 Q584.9 469.0 701.0 546.4\" stroke=\"#588d9e\" stroke-width=\".6\" opacity=\".13\" fill=\"none\"/>\n<circle cx=\"468.9\" cy=\"461.5\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#8cafbc\" opacity=\".4\"/>\n<path d=\"M780.7 425.6 Q680.5 453.3 580.3 550.9\" stroke=\"#588d9e\" stroke-width=\".6\" opacity=\".13\" fill=\"none\"/>\n<path d=\"M780.7 425.6 Q600.0 368.0 419.3 380.4\" stroke=\"#588d9e\" stroke-width=\".6\" opacity=\".13\" fill=\"none\"/>\n<circle cx=\"780.7\" cy=\"425.6\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#8cafbc\" opacity=\".4\"/>\n<path d=\"M580.3 550.9 Q558.3 429.0 536.4 377.1\" stroke=\"#588d9e\" stroke-width=\".6\" opacity=\".1